1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,279
Look at this java for a moment. No
@ComponentScan. No stereotype on
TicketService.

2
00:00:08,399 --> 00:00:19,640
The full-screen cinema box office wires
every collaborator in one @Configuration
class so the seating map never appears
twice by accident and ops can read the
graph top to bottom before Friday night
rush.

3
00:00:19,759 --> 00:00:24,439
@Configuration marks a class as a source
of bean definitions via @Bean methods.

4
00:00:24,559 --> 00:00:35,839
In full (default) mode, Spring enhances
the configuration class — historically
with CGLIB — so calls between @Bean
methods on the same class do not create a
second instance.

5
00:00:35,959 --> 00:00:43,999
When ticketService() calls seatMap(), that
call is intercepted and returns the
singleton already managed by the
container, not a fresh SeatMap.load(...).

6
00:00:44,119 --> 00:00:57,919
That interception is the whole point of
default @Configuration semantics. Walk
each method. seatMap() loads auditorium
JSON once when the bean is first created;
method name seatMap becomes the default
bean name.

7
00:00:58,039 --> 00:01:11,879
pricingPolicy() returns a matinee policy
singleton. ticketService() looks like
ordinary Java calling two neighbors —
under enhancement it is not: each nested
call asks the container for the singleton
of that bean name.

8
00:01:11,999 --> 00:01:21,759
paymentGateway(Environment env) uses a
different injection style: method
*parameters* are satisfied from the
container (Environment is a built-in
bean),

9
00:01:21,879 --> 00:01:26,079
then the method body constructs the card-
present gateway with a required property.

10
00:01:26,199 --> 00:01:38,719
No sibling call needed. Runtime refresh
for the cinema app, in order. Context
registers CinemaTicketingConfig as a
configuration class and enhances it when
proxyBeanMethods remains true (the
default).

11
00:01:38,839 --> 00:01:45,119
It derives bean definitions named seatMap,
pricingPolicy, ticketService,
paymentGateway.

12
00:01:45,239 --> 00:01:58,359
At end of refresh (or on first demand),
instantiating ticketService invokes the
enhanced method; inside, seatMap() and
pricingPolicy() resolve through the
interceptor to container-managed
singletons — one JSON load,

13
00:01:58,479 --> 00:01:59,759
one policy.

14
00:01:59,879 --> 00:02:02,879
Cashiers in the same JVM share that seat
map.

15
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:13,280
You still need session-scoped or request-
scoped hold state elsewhere if reserved
seats are per guest; configuration mode
only guarantees singleton *definition*
semantics for these beans,

16
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:16,400
not business-level locking across kiosks.

17
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:28,400
Lite mode flips the footgun back on.
@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false) —
or plain @Bean methods on a class
*without* @Configuration — skips
enhancement.

18
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:33,000
Then ticketService() calling seatMap()
truly executes SeatMap.load again.

19
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:47,759
Symptom: two SeatMap instances in memory;
cashier A reserves seat H12 on map
instance 1; cashier B still sees H12 free
on map instance 2; double-booking until
something hits the database constraint.

20
00:02:47,879 --> 00:02:56,599
Another symptom in tests: asserting
ticketService and a separately injected
SeatMap are == fails under lite mode and
passes under full mode.

21
00:02:56,719 --> 00:03:06,520
Fix when you want lite mode for faster
startup: do not call sibling @Bean methods
— inject collaborators as method
parameters: Look at this java for a
moment.

22
00:03:06,639 --> 00:03:20,599
Failure mode without lite mode involved:
forgetting @Configuration entirely and
only using @Bean on a @Component class —
methods may still register beans, but
inter-bean calls are plain Java, same
split-brain risk.

23
00:03:20,719 --> 00:03:29,759
Symptom often appears only under
concurrent kiosk use, not in a single-
threaded unit test that never compares
identities. Trade-offs.

24
00:03:29,879 --> 00:03:39,159
Full @Configuration makes method-call
wiring safe and readable for small graphs;
enhancement adds a proxy/subclass and a
bit of startup cost.

25
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:47,000
Lite mode and parameter injection scale
better for large configs and avoid proxy
surprises, at the cost of losing "just
call the method" style.

26
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:57,200
Explicit @Configuration without component
scanning maximizes auditability for
regulated box-office systems; it costs a
registration line whenever a new service
appears.

27
00:03:57,319 --> 00:04:10,799
Misconception unique to @Configuration: "A
@Configuration class is just a factory
with annotations; method calls behave like
ordinary Java." In default proxy-bean-
methods mode they do not. That is the
feature.

28
00:04:10,919 --> 00:04:23,719
If you need ordinary Java call semantics,
set proxyBeanMethods = false and inject
collaborators as method parameters instead
of calling sibling @Bean methods. Cinema
wiring is explicit and readable.

29
00:04:23,839 --> 00:04:30,159
The SMS confirmation vendor SDK still
cannot be annotated @Component because the
cinema does not own that JAR.

30
00:04:30,279 --> 00:04:37,839
Registering foreign objects needs the
@Bean method pattern as a first-class tool
— not only inside cozy configuration
demos.
